# Veripsa release gate (auto-discovered). One gate per file -> two PRs adding gates add two FILES, never a
# shared line -> no false merge conflict on registration. register_gate is defined in run_gates.sh.
# Format: register_gate <test> <marker> <name> <ok_blurb> <desc> [tail]
register_gate "tests/test_webhook_no_hang.py" "WEBHOOK NO-HANG GATE: PASS" "webhook never hangs reading a live-socket body (prod launch-blocker regression)" "WEBHOOK NO-HANG: the public webhook receiver must NEVER hang reading a request body off a LIVE socket. Regression guard for the production launch-blocker where EVERY webhook POST timed out: read_bounded_body did rfile.read(cap+1) (cap = 2 MiB), which on a real keep-alive socket BLOCKS waiting for 2 MiB of bytes an HTTP client never sends (it holds the connection open for the response -- no EOF), so every GitHub delivery failed AND the single-threaded server's /healthz was starved -> restart loop. The unit tests missed it because they fed io.BytesIO (instant EOF -> read(cap+1) returns); a real socket does not. This gate uses a REAL socketpair (no EOF until close): (1) NO HANG -- body sent + write end left OPEN -> read_bounded_body RETURNS PROMPTLY with exactly the declared body (the old read(cap+1) would block); (2) truncation still caught (fewer bytes than declared, then EOF -> 400); (3) an over-cap declared Content-Length is 413'd header-only WITHOUT touching the socket; (4) STRUCTURAL: serve() binds a ThreadingHTTPServer (one slow request can't starve /healthz) and sets a per-request Handler.timeout (a slowloris read is bounded). The fix reads EXACTLY the declared length (already <= cap, so the memory-DoS bound holds; a lying-small length reads only those bytes and the HMAC rejects the short body)" "webhook no-hang: read_bounded_body returns on a live keep-alive socket (not read(cap+1) which hung every POST), truncation->400, over-cap->413 header-only, threading server + per-request timeout" 22
